AJAX Summary (ASP.NET MVC)
What is AJAX?

AJAX (Asynchronous JavaScript and XML) is a technique that allows web pages to communicate with the server without reloading the entire page.

Although AJAX originally stood for Asynchronous JavaScript and XML, today it commonly uses JSON instead of XML because JSON is lightweight and easier to work with.

Without AJAX
User Clicks Button
       ↓
Browser Sends Request
       ↓
Server Processes Request
       ↓
Entire Page Reloads
With AJAX
User Clicks Button
       ↓
AJAX Sends Request
       ↓
Server Returns Data (JSON)
       ↓
Only Required Part Updates
Why Use AJAX?
Faster user experience
No full page refresh
Reduces server load
Better performance
Smooth UI interactions
Fetch data dynamically
How AJAX Works
Browser
   │
   │ Click Event
   ▼
JavaScript / jQuery AJAX
   │
HTTP Request
   ▼
MVC Controller
   │
Business Logic
   ▼
Database
   │
JSON Response
   ▼
JavaScript Updates HTML
AJAX Request Types
Method	Purpose
GET	Retrieve data
POST	Insert data
PUT	Update data
DELETE	Delete data
jQuery AJAX Syntax
$.ajax({
    url: "/Employee/GetEmployees",
    type: "GET",
    success: function(result){
        console.log(result);
    },
    error:function(err){
        console.log(err);
    }
});
ASP.NET MVC Example
Model
public class Employee
{
    public int Id { get; set; }

    public string Name { get; set; }

    public string Department { get; set; }
}
Controller
public class EmployeeController : Controller
{
    public JsonResult GetEmployees()
    {
        List<Employee> employees = new List<Employee>()
        {
            new Employee(){ Id=1, Name="John", Department="IT"},
            new Employee(){ Id=2, Name="David", Department="HR"}
        };

        return Json(employees, JsonRequestBehavior.AllowGet);
    }
}
View
<button id="btnLoad">Load Employees</button>

<div id="result"></div>

<script>

$("#btnLoad").click(function(){

    $.ajax({

        url:'/Employee/GetEmployees',

        type:'GET',

        success:function(data){

            var html="";

            $.each(data,function(i,item){

                html += item.Name + "<br>";

            });

            $("#result").html(html);

        }

    });

});

</script>
Flow
Button Click
      ↓
AJAX Call
      ↓
MVC Controller
      ↓
Database
      ↓
JSON
      ↓
Update Div
20 ASP.NET MVC AJAX Interview Questions & Answers
1. What is AJAX?

Answer:
AJAX is a technique used to send and receive data from the server asynchronously without refreshing the whole page.

2. What does AJAX stand for?

Answer:

Asynchronous JavaScript And XML.

Nowadays JSON is used more often than XML.

3. Why do we use AJAX in MVC?

Answer:

Faster page updates
Better user experience
Partial page refresh
Less bandwidth usage
4. Which HTTP methods are commonly used in AJAX?

Answer:

GET
POST
PUT
DELETE

Example:

type:"GET"

or

type:"POST"
5. What data format is commonly used with AJAX?

Answer:

JSON.

Example:

{
   "Id":1,
   "Name":"John"
}
6. How do you return JSON from an MVC controller?

Answer:

return Json(employee, JsonRequestBehavior.AllowGet);
7. Why is JsonRequestBehavior.AllowGet used?

Answer:

In classic ASP.NET MVC, GET requests returning JSON are blocked by default for security. AllowGet explicitly permits JSON responses to GET requests.

8. What is the difference between GET and POST in AJAX?

Answer:

GET	POST
Retrieves data	Sends data
Parameters in URL	Parameters in request body
Can be cached	Typically not cached
Used for reading	Used for creating/submitting
9. How do you send data using AJAX POST?

Answer:

$.ajax({

    url:'/Employee/Add',

    type:'POST',

    data:{
        Name:'John',
        Department:'IT'
    }

});
10. How do you receive POST data in MVC?

Answer:

[HttpPost]
public ActionResult Add(Employee emp)
{
    return Json(true);
}
11. How do you call an MVC action using AJAX?

Answer:

$.ajax({

    url:'/Employee/GetEmployees',

    type:'GET'

});
12. How do you display AJAX data on the page?

Answer:

$("#result").html(data.Name);
13. What is the success callback?

Answer:

It executes when the server successfully returns a response.

success:function(data){

}
14. What is the error callback?

Answer:

It executes if the request fails due to server, network, or other errors.

error:function(error){

}
15. What is the difference between synchronous and asynchronous requests?

Answer:

Synchronous:

Browser waits for the response.
UI is blocked.

Asynchronous:

Browser continues working.
Response is handled later.

AJAX is asynchronous by default.

16. How do you pass parameters to an MVC action using AJAX?

Answer:

$.ajax({

url:'/Employee/Get',

data:{ id:5 }

});

Controller:

public JsonResult Get(int id)
{
}
17. How do you reload only a specific <div> using AJAX?

Answer:

$("#EmployeeDiv").html(result);

Only that <div> is updated, not the entire page.

18. What are the advantages of AJAX?

Answer:

Faster UI
Better performance
Reduced server load
Partial updates
Improved user experience
19. What are some common AJAX errors?

Answer:

Incorrect URL
Controller action not found
HTTP 404 or 500 errors
JavaScript syntax errors
Invalid JSON response
Missing anti-forgery token for secure POST requests
20. Explain the complete AJAX flow in ASP.NET MVC.

Answer:

User clicks a button.
JavaScript triggers an AJAX request.
Request is sent to the MVC controller.
Controller processes the request (and may call the database).
Controller returns JSON or HTML.
JavaScript receives the response.
The page updates only the required section without a full refresh.
Real-Time Example: Search Employees Without Refresh
Controller
public JsonResult Search(string name)
{
    var employees = db.Employees
                      .Where(e => e.Name.Contains(name))
                      .ToList();

    return Json(employees, JsonRequestBehavior.AllowGet);
}
View
<input type="text" id="txtSearch" />

<button id="btnSearch">Search</button>

<div id="result"></div>

<script>

$("#btnSearch").click(function(){

    $.ajax({

        url:'/Employee/Search',

        type:'GET',

        data:{
            name:$("#txtSearch").val()
        },

        success:function(data){

            var html="";

            $.each(data,function(i,item){

                html += "<p>" + item.Name + "</p>";

            });

            $("#result").html(html);

        }

    });

});

</script>
